# Wider ordinary FP16 entries: CPU ABI proof and bounded probe proposal

This is a staged CPU investigation of the unchanged original C64, C128 and
C256 ordinary window entries. It does not execute CUDA, invoke the DLL host,
establish wider FP16 numerical parity, or measure either implementation. The
existing C32 probe is the only completed native FP16 runtime result: its 16
fixture/phase cases and 96 replays passed byte comparison under memcheck using
the installed v24 extension. That result cannot be generalized to these widths.

## Entry and allocation contract

| Channels | Native module / symbol | Block | Static shared bytes | CTA barriers |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|
| 64 | module1 / `cc_tinlayout_fused_swin_2h_64_2` | `(32,2,1)` | 8,192 | 6 |
| 128 | module2 / `cc_tinlayout_fused_swin_4h_128_4` | `(32,4,1)` | 16,384 | 10 |
| 256 | module3 / `cc_tinlayout_fused_swin_8h_256_8` | `(32,8,1)` | 32,768 | 8 |

All three entries have an 88-byte argument record. Input, output and weight
pointers occupy bytes 0, 8, 16. Byte 24 is an unused pointer slot, initialized to
zero in the proposed record. Height, width and signed x/y origins occupy
bytes 32, 36, 40, 44. Remaining bytes are zero. No dynamic shared allocation is
requested. The four origins are `(0,0)`, `(-4,-4)`, `(-4,0)`, `(0,-4)`;
grid dimensions are `ceil((W-origin_x)/8)` by `ceil((H-origin_y)/8)`.

The proof is restricted to positive 4-aligned H/W between 8 and 64, and its
enumerated cases use 8x8, 12x20, 16x24 in all four phases. It does not justify
transition blocks, down/up sampling entries, nonordinary weight records,
unbounded dimensions, or global-attention scratch protocols.

Pinned original artifacts:

| C | PTX SHA256 | Cubin SHA256 |
|---|---|---|
| 64 | `0ebd93ef1f85ac4b1981a2dc31e49c00273e107ebe397bb13b0a72bc97face99` | `a10a8083b9489622fe290d669f05f38db7308c3c10771d0f0d6f22718e3cb1ae` |
| 128 | `ea5afe1ed2dbfb125a91db607210c6905632e047e22d40f7f2ffdb1bd77e8a3e` | `9cba6bc0001a80c902ee20fd5638bf442bdf0674aaada676d67a6d1b89ca0aaa` |
| 256 | `fc7b019606d6f00a3dad501b39ec7a38fe0d904b9f187b0254b92935d40565b5` | `46ad7753bfb3a70a92a3524a5e638bb2fe93edb37cf3389ad1fa524e4e19b084` |

The tool pins both each complete PTX and the exact entry-body hash. It rejects
unexpected parameter sizes, shared allocations, local-memory operations and
atomics. Cubin hashes above identify the proposed future runtime binaries;
the CPU address proof interprets PTX, not those cubins.

## Half image layout

Each 4x4 image tile occupies `32*C` bytes. Inside a tile, each group of 16
channels occupies a 512-byte C-fragment plane. For tile-local token `m` and
channel `n`:

```
lane = (m % 8)*4 + (n % 8)//2
fragment = ((n//8)%2)*4 + ((m//8)%2)*2 + n%2
byte = (n//16)*512 + lane*16 + fragment*2
```

The full tile index is `(y//4)*(W//4)+x//4`, with `m=(y%4)*4+x%4`.
The enumeration proves this map is bijective and that actual original global
addresses read every input byte and write every output byte exactly once for
each tested case. The original cooperative readers differ: C64 distributes
input tiles between its two warps; C128/C256 distribute channel groups. This
is why simply copying the C32 thread mapping was insufficient.

Address coverage and bijection are safety and layout evidence. They do not
alone prove that native arithmetic assigns the intended logical value to each
location; the identity and nonuniform runtime fixtures remain necessary.

## Derived Half weight records

Only the original `WEIGHTS_HT` resource is available. These records are a
proposed conversion of its decoded E4 values to Half, using natural input
channels and the native K16 B-fragment order. They are not a separately
discovered original FP16 checkpoint or a verified DLL host conversion.

| Region / byte offset | C64 | C128 | C256 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Grouped W1 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Grouped W2 | 32,768 | 131,072 | 524,288 |
| W3 | 49,152 | 163,840 | 589,824 |
| FFN scale | 57,360 | 196,624 | 720,912 |
| QKV | 57,504 | 196,896 | 721,440 |
| Relative bias | 82,080 | 295,200 | 1,114,656 |
| Float head scale | 98,464 | 327,968 | 1,180,192 |
| Projection | 98,480 | 327,984 | 1,180,224 |
| Attention scale | 106,672 | 360,752 | 1,311,296 |
| Logical read end | 106,800 | 361,008 | 1,311,808 |
| Guarded payload allocation | 106,816 | 361,024 | 1,311,808 |

W1 has logical shape `[C/32,C,128]`, W2`[C/32,128,32]`, W3 and projection
`[C,C]`, QKV`[C,3C]`. Matrix bytes double when converting E4 to Half;
Half scales/biases and Float head scales preserve their source bits. The
16-byte separators and head-scale padding follow the original PTX offsets.

The scalar Half B coordinate map is:

```
tile = (k//16)*(N//16) + n//16
lane = (n%8)*4 + (k%8)//2
index = tile*256 + lane*8 + ((n//8)%2)*4 + ((k//8)%2)*2 + k%2
```

For the first complete CTA of each width, actual original weight reads cover
exactly the proposed matrix/auxiliary regions, with no padding reads:106,760,
360,976 and 1,311,776 distinct bytes. The grouped matrix records are contiguous
K rows with group boundaries at multiples of 16, so their local fragment order
is unchanged by concatenation. A separate CPU decoder reconstructs all 54
logical tensors from blocks 5/63, 9/57, 15/49 and matches the existing independent
checkpoint decoder exactly, including group axes, relative bias and head scales.

This validates the proposed conversion and actual load-region coverage. It
does not replace native numerical comparison or prove the unavailable host
conversion algorithm.

## Address/control interpreter and evidence

`tools/vendor_fp16_window_proof.py.draft` parses every instruction and follows
only known integer addresses and control. Tensor data stays unknown. Every
unknown instruction invalidates its destinations, including destinations that
previously held known integers. Unknown addresses and branch predicates fail
the proof. Backward branches have a bounded step limit.

An optional conservative register slice retains every global/shared access,
every branch and barrier, and every dynamic definition of registers that can
reach them. It does not assume register names are SSA. Full and sliced traces
are compared at boundary CTAs for both the first and last warp/lane of every
width and phase.

All memory accesses must be aligned and inside their exact allocation.
Global writes can target only output. Every CTA thread must encounter the
same sequence of barriers. Shared reads require an earlier initialized byte;
cross-thread read/write overlap and overlapping writers within one barrier
epoch are rejected. Same-thread reads after its own writes are allowed.

The draft test suite currently passes 11 tests (with width/phase subcases),
including malicious unknown destinations, missing initialization, shared
write overlap, read/write hazards, bad alignment, malformed dimensions and
independent real-weight decoding. The full 36-case address sweep passed:
three shapes, four phases and three widths. Its report is saved at
`outputs/vendor_fp16_window_address_proof.json.draft`. The exact weight-byte
coverage and maximum read end remain identical across all 12 cases of each
width. No runtime helper is enabled by this CPU result.

Independent review found no blocker. It compared 480 additional full/sliced
thread traces across all widths and phases, four boundary CTAs at 12x20,
the first/last warp, and lanes 0, 1, 15, 16 and 31. All memory events and
barrier sequences agreed. The reviewer also confirmed that every observed
memory instruction has the modeled 4/16-byte width, and all live integer
multiply/MAD instructions use the supported low/wide semantics. Input-read
totals equal unique input coverage in every saved case.

## Next guarded runtime calibration

`tools/vendor_fp16_window_probe.py.draft` passed independent CPU review and
has not executed CUDA. The default path performs only CPU asset/proof
checks. An explicit `--execute` switch is required to launch a native entry.
The helper pins the reviewed proof script hash as well as original cubin,
PTX, DLL and resource hashes. Seven additional CPU tests pass, including
all fixture roundtrips, real-weight cross-checks, every grouped FFN expert,
W3 channel routing, and QKV's correct `[head,3,32]` column order. The initial
CPU-only smoke report is `outputs/vendor_fp16_window_probe_cpu_smoke.json.draft`.

The staged helper preserves the following reviewed contract:

1. Verify DLL resource, original cubin, helper, extension and candidate source
   hashes. Require SM120 for the unchanged original binaries.
2. Validate the exact requested small shape/phase with this CPU interpreter
   before creating any native allocation. Start with ordinary blocks 5, 9, 15;
   include decoder-side blocks 63, 57, 49 as independent real-weight cases.
3. Guard input/output/derived weights, and all externally supplied candidate
   tensors. Check every guard and immutable payload after every verified replay
   and after capture warmup.
4. Use identity residual, grouped FFN basis, attention basis and actual
   nonuniform weights; map input/output with the explicit Half map above.
   The FFN fixture must exercise each expert and W3, not reuse C32's two-layer
   fixture unchanged. Nonzero Q/K prevents degenerate normalization tests.
5. Compare every published Half byte with the unchanged FP16 candidate.
   Preserve the candidate arithmetic and report mismatches without loosening
   comparison thresholds. The earlier PTX-only normalization discrepancy was
   disproved by original SASS and the unchanged C32 runtime result.
6. Poison native output before every ordinary replay, and both native and
   retained candidate outputs before every graph replay. Then recheck output,
   all guards and all immutable payloads. Run
   memcheck first; add bounded racecheck for the shared-memory native entries.

A successful result would establish only these ordinary entries, derived
weights and bounded fixtures. Speed comparison, the DLL host path, transition
blocks and the full FP16 trunk remain separate work.
